.TH DE25-FLASH 8 "November 2026" "de25-nano-fpga-tools" "DE25-Nano"
.SH NAME
de25-flash \- write the DE25-Nano's flash, which holds the HPS image
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B de25-flash status
.br
.B de25-flash write
.RB [ \-\-force ]
.RI [ name ]
.br
.B de25-flash restore
.RB [ \-\-force ]
.RI [ file ]
.br
.B de25-flash sync
.SH DESCRIPTION
The DE25-Nano's flash chip holds what the board runs first.
As the board is shipped, that is Terasic's whole FPGA design with a boot loader.
For Altera's "HPS boot first" mode, which this system uses, it is the
.IR "HPS image" :
the settings of the SoC (its pins and memory, and which of its peripherals are routed to
the FPGA) and U-Boot's first stage, the SPL.
The FPGA's logic is then a core image on the SD card, chosen with
.BR de25-fpga (8).
.PP
A
.I boot package
is everything that runs before the kernel, built together: the HPS image
.RB ( hps.rpd ),
which goes in the flash, and
.B u-boot.itb
and
.BR boot.scr ,
which go on the card's FAT partition,
.IR /boot/firmware .
A boot package is named
.BI de25-nano-boot- name
and is kept in
.BI /usr/lib/de25-nano/boot/ name\fR.
Installing or upgrading the package does not write the flash.
.B de25-flash write
does, and nothing else does.
.PP
The chip takes up new flash contents only at power-on.
After the flash has been written, the board's power must be unplugged and plugged in
again; a restart is not enough, and must not be tried.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B status
Show whether the SoC has access to the flash, what the flash holds, whether
.B u-boot.itb
and
.B boot.scr
on the card are those of an installed boot package, whether a flash write is waiting or
has failed, and the saved flash contents on the card.
What the flash holds is shown only to root, who alone can read it.
.TP
.BR write " [\fIname\fR]"
Put the boot package
.I name
into effect: write its HPS image to the flash and put its
.B u-boot.itb
and
.B boot.scr
on the card.
.I name
may be left out when one boot package is installed.
The command says what it will do and asks first.
.IP
On a board whose flash holds the factory contents, Linux cannot reach the flash.
The command then leaves the HPS image on the card, and U-Boot writes it at the next boot:
it checks the image, saves what the flash held to the card as
.BI saved- crc32 .rpd\fR,
writes, and compares.
This takes about two minutes, and the LED beside the HPS KEY button shows how it is going:
one second on and one second off while it works, steady on when it has finished, four
blinks a second if it failed.
Copy the saved file to another computer as well.
.IP
On a board whose flash already holds an HPS image, Linux writes the flash directly.
What the flash held is not saved.
.IP
If the flash already holds exactly the HPS image to be written, the flash is left alone
and only the two files on the card are renewed.
.TP
.BR restore " [\fIfile\fR]"
Write saved flash contents back, from Linux, and stop a core being loaded at boot.
Without
.I file
it takes the saved file on the card, if there is exactly one.
The file must be the whole flash, 16 MiB.
With the factory contents back and Terasic's card in the board, the board is as it came.
.TP
.B sync
Renew
.B u-boot.itb
and
.B boot.scr
on the card from the installed boot package, but only if the flash holds exactly that
package's HPS image.
It never writes the flash.
It is run when a boot package is installed or upgraded, so an upgrade that leaves the HPS
image unchanged takes effect at the next boot, and one that changes it waits for
.BR "de25-flash write" .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-force
With
.B write
and
.BR restore :
do not ask.
.SH FILES
.TP
.BI /usr/lib/de25-nano/boot/ name /hps.rpd
The HPS image, as raw bytes from the start of the flash.
.TP
.BI /usr/lib/de25-nano/boot/ name /u-boot.itb
U-Boot with TF-A, which the SPL loads from the card.
.TP
.BI /usr/lib/de25-nano/boot/ name /boot.scr
The script U-Boot runs. It writes the flash when asked, and loads the selected core image.
.TP
.I /dev/mtd0
The flash, when the chip has handed it to the SoC. Not there with the factory contents.
.TP
.IR /boot/firmware/write-on-boot.rpd ", " /boot/firmware/write-on-boot.txt
A flash write waiting for the next boot: the new contents, and their checksum.
.TP
.BI /boot/firmware/saved- crc32 .rpd
What the flash held before U-Boot wrote it.
.TP
.I /boot/firmware/write-on-boot-failed.txt
Left by U-Boot if the write failed: the step it failed in.
.TP
.I /boot/firmware/u-boot-previous.itb
The
.B u-boot.itb
that was on the card before the last change.
.SH EXAMPLES
A new board, with its factory flash contents: write the flash, and have the base core
loaded afterwards.
.PP
.nf
.RS
sudo de25-flash write
sudo de25-fpga select base
sudo reboot
.RE
.fi
.PP
Then watch the LED, and when it is steady on, unplug the power and plug it in again.
.PP
Going back to the factory contents:
.PP
.nf
.RS
sudo de25-flash restore
sudo systemctl halt
.RE
.fi
.PP
Then unplug the power and plug it in again.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR de25-fpga (8),
.BR flashcp (8)
